# Destroyer sound circuits -- netlist converted from MAME
#
# Ryan Holtz's netlist of the sound circuits on the game board, which his file says is
# derived from the schematics in the manual. The game logic's sound-enable and sound-data
# lines arrive as ports (MAME's TTL_INPUT names), and so do the video timing signals the
# sounds are clocked from, which MAME runs as ideal clocks.
#
# Source: MAME mame0289 (commit f34f02505e32), src/mame/atari/nl_destroyr.cpp,
# NETLIST_START(destroyr). Licence BSD-3-Clause by the file's own header (line 1),
# copyright-holders Ryan Holtz (line 2).
# https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mamedev/mame/mame0289/src/mame/atari/nl_destroyr.cpp
# Converted by tools/mame_netlist.py; checked by tools/build_board_nets.py.
#
# What the conversion means. MAME's netlists are written to be simulated, so this is a
# second reading of the sheet by someone else, not a read of it: designators and pin
# numbers are MAME's unless the notes say the sheet was read to correct them. Names on
# `label` lines are MAME's, which mostly follow the sheet's with Q for an overbar; `net`
# is used only for the supply rails. A `port` is a net that leaves what this file
# describes -- a signal from the CPU or the other board, a cabinet control, or a source
# MAME simulates with a stand-in rather than the circuit.
#
#   net NAME: PIN ...     a supply rail
#   label NAME: PIN ...   a net MAME names
#   wire PIN PIN ...      an unnamed connection
#   port NAME: PIN ...    a net that leaves this file (see above)
#   nc PIN ...            a pin with nothing attached
#   parallel PIN ...      outputs the board sums on one node by design
#   value REF VALUE       a passive's value, in the unit MAME's own macro uses
#
# PIN is DESIGNATOR.NUMBER. Diode pin 1 is the cathode; transistors keep B, C and E;
# MAME does not record which way round an electrolytic goes, so C pins are MAME's 1/2.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Undone: what MAME does to simulate the board that the board does not do
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# line 162: dropped NET_C(GND, IC_L7.1, IC_L7.2) -- these gates are used elsewhere on the board (sheets 3-5), not tied off
# line 163: dropped NET_C(GND, IC_L7.4, IC_L7.5) -- these gates are used elsewhere on the board (sheets 3-5), not tied off
# line 165: dropped NET_C(GND, IC_M8.4, IC_M8.5) -- these gates are used elsewhere on the board (sheets 3-5), not tied off
# line 166: dropped NET_C(GND, IC_M8.9, IC_M8.10) -- these gates are used elsewhere on the board (sheets 3-5), not tied off
# line 167: dropped NET_C(GND, IC_M8.12, IC_M8.13) -- these gates are used elsewhere on the board (sheets 3-5), not tied off
# line 171: dropped NET_C(GND, IC_K9.1, IC_K9.5, IC_K9.9, IC_K9.11, IC_K9.13) -- these gates are used elsewhere on the board (sheets 3-5), not tied off
# line 164: NET_C(GND, IC_L7.9, IC_L7.10) -> nc -- tied to GND in MAME's "Tie off unused gates and pins" block
# line 168: NET_C(GND, IC_A9.1, IC_A9.2) -> nc -- tied to GND in MAME's "Tie off unused gates and pins" block
# line 169: NET_C(GND, IC_A9.9, IC_A9.10) -> nc -- tied to GND in MAME's "Tie off unused gates and pins" block
# line 170: NET_C(GND, IC_A9.12, IC_A9.13) -> nc -- tied to GND in MAME's "Tie off unused gates and pins" block
# line 172: NET_C(GND, IC_H8.5, IC_H8.9) -> nc -- tied to GND in MAME's "Tie off unused gates and pins" block
# line 173: NET_C(VCC, IC_H8.4, IC_H8.10) -> nc -- tied to +5V in MAME's "Tie off unused gates and pins" block
#
# Corrected from sheet 4 of the schematic (TM-106 p. 37) and sheets 3 and 5:
#   UK8: the sheet's part is 7428, MAME's 7402 -- sheet 4 prints '28' in all four gates, and the parts list has a 7428 at K8; MAME's comment agrees ('Should be a 7428'), and its 7402 has the same pins
#   UL9: the sheet's part is 74LS164, MAME's 74164 -- sheet 4 and the parts list
#   UM9: the sheet's part is 74LS164, MAME's 74164 -- sheet 4 and the parts list
#   sheet 5: K9 pin 1 is used there, not by the sound: UK9.1 (K9_1)
#   sheet 5: K9 pin 2 is used there, not by the sound: UK9.2 (K9_2)
#   sheet 5: K9 pin 5 is used there, not by the sound: UK9.5 (K9_5)
#   sheet 5: K9 pin 6 is used there, not by the sound: UK9.6 (K9_6)
#   sheet 4's output-strobe decode (ABUS3 in, out to E8 pin 5): K9 pin 9 is used there, not by the sound: UK9.9 (K9_9)
#   sheet 4's output-strobe decode (ABUS3 in, out to E8 pin 5): K9 pin 8 is used there, not by the sound: UK9.8 (K9_8)
#   sheet 3 (H7/J7 to the H4/J4 strobes): K9 pin 13 is used there, not by the sound: UK9.13 (K9_13)
#   sheet 3 (H7/J7 to the H4/J4 strobes): K9 pin 12 is used there, not by the sound: UK9.12 (K9_12)
#   sheet 3 (H7/J7 to the H4/J4 strobes): K9 pin 11 is used there, not by the sound: UK9.11 (K9_11)
#   sheet 3 (H7/J7 to the H4/J4 strobes): K9 pin 10 is used there, not by the sound: UK9.10 (K9_10)
#   sheet 5's video summing: M8 pin 4 is used there, not by the sound: UM8.4 (M8_4)
#   sheet 5's video summing: M8 pin 5 is used there, not by the sound: UM8.5 (M8_5)
#   sheet 5's video summing: M8 pin 6 is used there, not by the sound: UM8.6 (M8_6)
#   sheet 5's video summing: M8 pin 9 is used there, not by the sound: UM8.9 (M8_9)
#   sheet 5's video summing: M8 pin 10 is used there, not by the sound: UM8.10 (M8_10)
#   sheet 5's video summing: M8 pin 8 is used there, not by the sound: UM8.8 (M8_8)
#   sheet 5's video summing: M8 pin 12 is used there, not by the sound: UM8.12 (M8_12)
#   sheet 5's video summing: M8 pin 13 is used there, not by the sound: UM8.13 (M8_13)
#   sheet 5's video summing: M8 pin 11 is used there, not by the sound: UM8.11 (M8_11)
#   sheet 5's object video: L7 pin 1 is used there, not by the sound: UL7.1 (L7_1)
#   sheet 5's object video: L7 pin 2 is used there, not by the sound: UL7.2 (L7_2)
#   sheet 5's object video: L7 pin 3 is used there, not by the sound: UL7.3 (L7_3)
#   sheet 5's object video: L7 pin 4 is used there, not by the sound: UL7.4 (L7_4)
#   sheet 5's object video: L7 pin 5 is used there, not by the sound: UL7.5 (L7_5)
#   sheet 5's object video: L7 pin 6 is used there, not by the sound: UL7.6 (L7_6)
# line 156: UC9 is MAME's LM324_VM5V12 model, MAME's LM324 with output limits for the -5V/+12V supply it runs on here; the part is the LM324
# line 157: UH9 is MAME's LM324_VM5V12 model, MAME's LM324 with output limits for the -5V/+12V supply it runs on here; the part is the LM324
# Stand-ins that are not parts (the nets they drive are ports): CLOCK(H256), CLOCK(V8), TTL_INPUT(MOTOR_SPEED), TTL_INPUT(NOISE), TTL_INPUT(ATTRACT), TTL_INPUT(SONGATE), TTL_INPUT(LAUNCH), TTL_INPUT(EXPLO), TTL_INPUT(SONLAT), TTL_INPUT(HE), TTL_INPUT(LE)
#
# Left out by MAME (commented out at TAG, so not in this file either):
#   line 126: CAP(C81, CAP_U(0.1))
#
# Compared with the schematic, sheet 4 of 5 (TM-106 p. 37, a clean 300 ppi scan), with the
# other four sheets and the parts list (pp. 64-65), on 2026-10-20: all 64 of MAME's nets in
# the sound circuit agree with the sheet, pin for pin and value for value, and so do the
# supplies (the LM324s at C9 and H9 on +12V and -5V, from a 7812 and a 7905; the MC3340 at
# D10 on +5V and -5V). The ports are the sheet's: F8 (a 9334) drives SONGATE, LAUNCH, EXPLO,
# SONLAT, HE and LE (addresses 1002-1007, HE 'high explosion', LE 'low explosion'); F7 (a
# 74174) drives MOTOR_SPEED and ATTRACT, and NOISE -- the shift registers' clear -- is F7 pin
# 5, which sheet 1's address map calls 'KICKER'.
#
# What MAME's 'Tie off unused gates and pins' block gets wrong is the board, not the sound:
# every K9 inverter, M8's gates on 4-6, 8-10 and 11-13, and L7's on 1-6 are used by the video
# and strobe logic on sheets 3-5, so they are ports here, not nc. The 556's RESETs (H8 4 and
# 10) and its outputs 5 and 9 are bare stubs on the sheet, as nc says; MAME ties the RESETs
# to +5V. A9's spare gates and L7 8-10 appear on no sheet (a visual sweep, not certain).
#
# On the sheet and not in this file: the decoupling capacitors C50, C60 (+12V), C54, C65 and
# C73 (-5V); a second, open jumper from the D10 pin 6 / Q4 node to the TDA1004's pin 5 and
# C83, beside the closed one this file has; and the TDA1004 amplifier itself (C81 from the
# volume pot's wiper, C82-C90, the speaker on edge pin 7). On all six electrolytics MAME's
# pin 1 is the + end. R78 has a pen scribble through its value; it reads 5.6K, and the parts
# list agrees.

# part UA9 7400
# part UC9 LM324
# part UD10 MC3340
# part UE9 CD4066
